        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "feat: expose UI diagnostics on the debug page",
        "body": "Closes #102689\nRelated: #49257\n\nAI-assisted: Codex.\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe authenticated debug page explains gateway state, but it does not expose the browser and UI environment that often determines whether a Control UI feature works. Diagnosing display, capability, or media-enumeration failures currently requires ad hoc browser inspection.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdd a deterministic UI Diagnostics table to the existing debug page. It reports a small allowlist of runtime, display, capability, and media signals with typed values and explicit statuses.\n\nThe collector is intentionally privacy-bounded: it does not read or render URLs, hosts, user-agent strings, tokens, storage, prompts, transcripts, device identifiers, device labels, or raw errors. Media inspection uses device enumeration only, never requests microphone access, and times out after 1.5 seconds instead of leaving the page stuck.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Makes UI/browser state visible in one inspectable table.\n- Keeps diagnostics readable at desktop and phone widths.\n- Uses semantic table headers, text status labels, polite loading state, and UI text-scale tokens.\n- Adds a manual refresh without prompting for browser permissions.\n- Localizes stable labels and formats dimensions, counts, and locale values through the existing i18n system.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Focused Vitest: 14 tests passed, including Chromium layout checks at 390 x 844 and 1440 x 900.\n- UI TypeScript check passed.\n- Control UI i18n drift check passed for all 20 generated locales.\n- Formatter check passed for the changed UI and i18n paths.\n- Production Control UI build passed.\n- Structured autoreview passed with no findings.\n- Live signed-in-profile screenshot is pending because the running Chrome profile did not expose a DevTools attachment endpoint; the PR remains a draft.\n",
